{
  "title": "Wildcard Matching",
  "description": "Decide whether a pattern `p` matches a string `s`. The pattern is made of lowercase letters and two wildcards:\n\n- `?` matches exactly one character, whatever it is.\n- `*` matches any run of characters, including an empty one.\n\nThe pattern must match the **whole** string. Return `true` if it does and `false` otherwise.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `0 <= s.size() <= 1000`, and `s` contains only lowercase English letters.\n- `0 <= p.size() <= 1000`, and `p` contains only lowercase English letters, `?`, and `*`.\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "bool wildcard_match(string s, string p) {\n    size_t i = 0, j = 0, star = string::npos, resume = 0;\n    while (i < s.size()) {\n        if (j < p.size() && (p[j] == '?' || p[j] == s[i])) {\n            ++i;\n            ++j;\n        } else if (j < p.size() && p[j] == '*') {\n            star = j++;\n            resume = i;\n        } else if (star != string::npos) {\n            j = star + 1;\n            i = ++resume;\n        } else {\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n    while (j < p.size() && p[j] == '*') {\n        ++j;\n    }\n    return j == p.size();\n}\n",
  "template": "bool wildcard_match(string s, string p) {\n    // Return whether the pattern p matches all of s.\n    return false;\n}\n",
  "tags": [
    "Strings",
    "Greedy",
    "Dynamic Programming"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "test_number": 0,
      "explanation": "The first * matches nothing, a matches a, the second * matches dce, and b matches the final b. The whole string is covered."
    },
    {
      "test_number": 1,
      "explanation": "? matches the c, but then the pattern needs an a and the string has a b. The match fails."
    }
  ],
